# shell: desktop
# splash: on
# The way out is the way in, run backwards.  The desktop does not simply
# stop being drawn: the black comes back down over it across the second
# it took to lift, holds, and the teardown then names each piece as it is
# put away -- so what a person sees is the machine closing rather than a
# window that vanished.
wait 150
expect-pixel 5 5 336698
note the boot screen is off and the desktop is up.  Now ask for it back,
note from the Deskbar's own row, and answer the question it asks
press 900 20 1
wait 2
release 900 20 1
wait 5
menu-pick Shut down
wait 5
expect-alert Shut down
key Return
note a second later the black has ARRIVED rather than the shell having
note gone: the backdrop's corner is under it, and the mark stands where
note it stood on the way in -- pinned on its filled face, the same pixel
note splash.script holds the way up by
wait 60
expect-pixel 5 5 000000
expect-pixel 507 307 f7e689
quit
